# motoquencer, fader mode 2: the CYAN turn-based gate-probability codes (#84).
# motoquencer.md, under the gate-probability table: "Gates depending on the turn
# (3, 5 and 6) are in cyan color and light steadily in the bars (turns) where
# they are on and blink in the other bars."
#
# Notch 6 (gateprob idx 5) plays every EVEN turn, notch 5 (idx 4) every ODD turn
# -- the same per-turn counter the gate decision uses (SeqCore::turnNotchPlays
# serves both, so the plate and the audible result can never disagree). The turn
# counter starts at 1, so the odd-turn step plays on the first run.
#
# SPEC-GAP: the off-turn blink rate is not in the manual. voidbot uses the
# SLOWEST code, 2 Hz at half duty -- the cyan colour already separates these
# notches from the random ones, so the blink only has to say "not this bar". At
# tickrate 100 that is 50 ticks: on for ticks 0..24 of each cycle, off for
# 25..49.
#
# The clock runs every 4 ticks from tick 5, so a turn is 16 ticks:
#   turn 1 (odd)  ticks  5..20   turn 3 (odd)  ticks 37..52
#   turn 2 (even) ticks 21..36   turn 4 (even) ticks 53..68
# and the assertions below sit on ticks where step 3 is the played one, leaving
# lanes 1, 2 and 4 free of the white marker.
tickrate 100
patch <<<
[m4]
[motoquencer]
    clock       = I1
    fadermode   = I2
    numsteps    = 4
    numfaders   = 4
    defaultgate = 1
    cv          = O1
    gate        = O2
>>>
# Fader mode 2; dial step 1 to notch 6 (idx 5, every EVEN turn) and step 2 to
# notch 5 (idx 4, every ODD turn). Steps 3 and 4 stay at the top notch 8, so
# they keep the steady blue and make the colour difference visible.
@0 set I2 2
@1 move F1 0.7142857
@2 move F2 0.5714286
# Both turn-based lanes are cyan, not blue -- before a single clock, on turn 1
# (odd): lane 2 plays this turn (steady), lane 1 does not (blinking, currently
# in its on phase, so both read lit here -- the colour is the point).
@3 expect F1.color 0.2 tol 1e-6
@3 expect F2.color 0.2 tol 1e-6
@3 expect F3.color 1.2 tol 1e-6
@3 expect F4.color 1.2 tol 1e-6
# Run the clock: 4 ticks per step.
@5 set I1 1
@6 set I1 0
@9 set I1 1
@10 set I1 0
@13 set I1 1
@14 set I1 0
@17 set I1 1
@18 set I1 0
# --- turn 2 (even) ---
@21 set I1 1
@22 set I1 0
@25 set I1 1
@26 set I1 0
@29 set I1 1
@30 set I1 0
# Tick 30: step 3 plays (white), and the 2 Hz blink is in its OFF phase. Turn 2
# is even, so lane 1 lights steadily and lane 2 -- off its turn -- is dark.
@30 expect F1.led 1 tol 1e-6
@30 expect F1.color 0.2 tol 1e-6
@30 expect F2.led 0 tol 1e-6
@30 expect F2.color 0.2 tol 1e-6
@30 expect F3.color -1 tol 1e-6
@30 expect F4.led 1 tol 1e-6
@30 expect F4.color 1.2 tol 1e-6
@33 set I1 1
@34 set I1 0
# --- turn 3 (odd) ---
@37 set I1 1
@38 set I1 0
@41 set I1 1
@42 set I1 0
@45 set I1 1
@46 set I1 0
# Tick 46: step 3 plays again, blink still in its off phase, but the turn is now
# ODD -- the exact mirror of tick 30.
@46 expect F1.led 0 tol 1e-6
@46 expect F1.color 0.2 tol 1e-6
@46 expect F2.led 1 tol 1e-6
@46 expect F2.color 0.2 tol 1e-6
@46 expect F4.led 1 tol 1e-6
@49 set I1 1
@50 set I1 0
# --- turn 4 (even) ---
@53 set I1 1
@54 set I1 0
@57 set I1 1
@58 set I1 0
@61 set I1 1
@62 set I1 0
# Tick 62: step 3 plays, turn 4 is even, and the blink is in its ON phase -- so
# the off-turn lane 2 is lit too. Half the cycle on, half off: that is what
# makes it read as a blink rather than as dark.
@62 expect F1.led 1 tol 1e-6
@62 expect F2.led 1 tol 1e-6
@62 expect F2.color 0.2 tol 1e-6
# A few ticks later the same lane 2 is dark again while lane 1 holds steady.
@75 expect F1.led 1 tol 1e-6
@75 expect F2.led 0 tol 1e-6
